---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import site from '../../site.config.json' with { type: 'json' };

const page = {
  url: '/about/', title: 'About Marine Diesel Engine Australia',
  seoTitle: 'About Marine Diesel Engine Australia', pageType: 'about',
  status: 'ready', author: site.defaultAuthor, reviewer: null,
  needsReviewer: false, parent: '/', parentTitle: 'Home',
  schema: ['AboutPage', 'BreadcrumbList'], updated: site.legalUpdated,
  indexable: true, blockers: [], faqs: [],
  headings: [
    { depth: 2, slug: 'editorial-team', text: 'Our editorial team' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'what-we-publish', text: 'What we publish' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'what-we-do-not-do', text: 'What we do not do' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'how-to-contact-us', text: 'How to contact us' },
  ],
  description: 'Learn who publishes Marine Diesel Engine Australia, what our independent engine guides cover and how we research, disclose and correct information.',
  primaryKeyword: 'about Marine Diesel Engine Australia', searchIntent: 'Navigational',
};
---
<Page page={page}>
  <h1>About Marine Diesel Engine Australia</h1>
  <p>
    Marine Diesel Engine Australia is an independent educational publication
    for Australian boat owners, buyers and operators. It turns common engine
    symptoms, service decisions and marine terminology into practical starting
    points, with safety limits and links to primary sources made clear.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editorial-team">Our editorial team</h2>
  <p>
    Articles are prepared under the organisational byline “The Marine Diesel
    Engine editorial team”. That byline describes a publishing function, not a
    named marine engineer or a claim of professional certification. Where a
    qualified person reviews a page, the reviewer will be identified on that
    page. A page without a named reviewer should not be read as technically
    certified.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site is powered by <strong>YouSail.com.au</strong>. YouSail provides the
    publishing platform and editorial support; the site does not operate a
    repair workshop or sell marine engines and parts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-we-publish">What we publish</h2>
  <p>
    The library covers marine diesel troubleshooting, cooling and exhaust
    systems, routine service work, buying and repowering decisions, engine
    brands, supplier categories and Australian location guides. Our aim is to
    help a reader ask better questions and recognise when professional help is
    required—not to replace the manual for a specific engine or vessel.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> for the source
    hierarchy, update process and use of publishing tools. The
    <a href="/resources/">authority resources</a> page links directly to
    manufacturers, regulators and safety organisations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-we-do-not-do">What we do not do</h2>
  <p>
    We do not diagnose individual engines, supervise repairs, warrant the work
    of listed businesses or guarantee that general information fits a
    particular installation. Prices and business details are published only
    when the source and context can be stated. Read the
    <a href="/disclaimer/">information disclaimer</a> before acting on a guide.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-contact-us">How to contact us</h2>
  <p>
    Use the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> to report a factual error,
    suggest a primary source or request a business-listing change. Our
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a> explains what evidence helps
    us assess a change quickly.
  </p>
</Page>
